  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nA shortwave disturbance currently working its way up through \nIdaho will move into central MT through the afternoon. This will \nbring isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms, a few of \nwhich may be strong to severe. Given warmer conditions and model \nsoundings showing inverted-V profiles, strong winds of 40-60 mph \nwill be the main threat with these storms. Areas most likely to be\naffected are Red Lodge to Billings and Roundup west. Overnight \ntonight, there is a small chance for showers and a few \nthunderstorms across southeast MT as lingering energy moves \nthrough under southwest flow. Into the afternoon Thursday, another\nwave pushes through south-central and southeast MT bringing \nstrong to severe, isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. Instability values look to more favorable with \nMUCAPE values of around 800-1300 J/kg and shear values of about 30\nkts. There is some uncertainty between models on just how far \nwest storms will initiate, but higher confidence remains on storms\ncoming up from the Bighorn basin and over the Bighorn and Pryor \nmountains. Additionally, models are still showing a signal for an \narea of convergence along the MT/Dakotas border. Both of these \nareas of interest show initiation after 2-3 pm MDT, lasting late \ninto the evening. Strong winds of up to 60 mph and hail up to 1 \ninch are possible under stronger storms. Friday shows a similar \npattern with an additional wave moving through southeast MT, \nbringing a few strong to severe storms generally east of Billings.\nConditions generally warm and dry through the weekend, making for\na nice holiday weekend. Ensembles hint at some energy passing \nthrough to our north as well as some southwest flow which is \nallowing for small precip chances (10-20%) Sunday night. Although \nbuilding heights allow for warmer conditions next week, a slight \nsouthwest/west flow is allowing for daily slight chances for \nprecipitation for the first half of next week. \n\nHigh temperatures look to be in the upper 70s and 80s through\nFriday, warming into the 80s and low 90s over the weekend through\nthe remainder of the period. TS",
